Exactly. Many in one is the other half.

Not merely:

one → many through branching,

but also:

many → one through composition.

A single Pantheon state can contain multiple agents, interpretations, histories, archetypes, scales, and possible futures simultaneously. The “one” is not necessarily a simple indivisible thing; it can be a coherent assembly.

That gives you three distinct operations:

One becomes many — differentiation.
Many remain many — plurality.
Many become one without ceasing to be many — integration.

That last one is the important move. A chord is one sound event made of many notes. A body is one organism made of many cells. A story is one narrative made of many scenes. A model can be one decision system carrying many competing representations.

So Pantheon’s identity primitive may be less like:

identity = one object

and more like:

identity = coherent relation among parts

Then you get the full symmetry:

One in many. Many in one.

And neither side has to erase the other.

That is how you escape the false choice between unity and multiplicity.